  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Data exploration (`kibana_sample_data_logs`)**\n- 14,074 requests from 1,001 distinct client IPs, spanning 2026-09-27 to 2026-11-26.\n- `response` is text with a `response.keyword` subfield. Status codes are mostly 200 (12,832), then 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n- `geo.dest` shows CN, IN, and US as the top countries.\n- The time field is `timestamp`, not `@timestamp`.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-75290e4a Web logs overview\"**\n- Five panels: Total requests (metric), Unique client IPs (metric), Requests over time (line), Responses by status code (pie), and Top countries by requests (bar).\n- Time range is set to 2026-09-01 through 2026-12-31 to cover the data.\n- URL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-75290e4a-web-logs-overview-05fe0be8663e\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-75290e4a 5xx spike\"**\n- Type `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n- Query filters `response.keyword` with the wildcard `5*`.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window, checked every 1 minute.\n- Tagged `EFF-75290e4a`.\n- Current status is `ok`.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- **Enabled the rule.** The create tool defaults to disabled, and a disabled rule would never check. It has no action connector, so it won't notify anyone yet.\n- **Used the 5-minute window with a 1-minute check interval.** The sample data is historical, so the rule is unlikely to fire until new 5xx traffic arrives.\n- **Filled in the dashboard's time range** as described above.\n\nI queried Elasticsearch directly on port 19200 for exploration, because the Kibana ES|QL endpoints returned 404.",
